      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-multiradiatus-bred/",
      "html": "<p>The breeding group of the fish described here originates from Peru.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-multiradiatus-bred/",
      "html": "<p>The care of all Brochis is easy and hardly differs from that of the remaining corys.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-multiradiatus-bred/",
      "html": "<p>Breeding of Brochis succeeds only rarely. Only B. splendens is bred in southeast Asia in good numbers, and an undescribed species from Bolivia, CW 035, was successfully bred by Hans-Georg Evers.</p><p>A breeder from Hungary, Pasaréti Gyula, achieved an extensive brood of Brochis multiradiatus from a group consisting of 10 males and 4 females. The young were only 2 months old and already 3-6 cm tall, and very attractively coloured.</p><p>Brochis species probably spawn exclusively in the rainy season, when ornamental fishing is impossible, and the young grow so fast that at the beginning of the dry season, when collectors can work again, they already look like adults.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/brochis-multiradiatus-bred/",
      "html": "<p>The big emerald corys of the genus Brochis clearly differ from all species of Corydoras through the long dorsal fin, which contains considerably more fin rays. While Corydoras have 7-8 rays in the dorsal fin, Brochis have 10-18.</p><p>The young of Brochis splendens look completely different from the adults. For the other Brochis species the juvenile form was previously unknown.</p>"
    }
  ],
